{
  "module": "Training: how the numbers are learned",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "tokenizer-fixed",
    "title": "The tokenizer is fixed, not learned",
    "summary": "Why the tokenizer is not one of the things training changes: it is built beforehand from corpus statistics, gradients cannot flow into how text is split, and only the looked-up embedding rows receive them.",
    "covers": ["tokenizer-not-trained"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "tokens",
        "reason": "The tokenizer is the thing in question."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "embeddings",
        "reason": "Only looked-up rows of the embedding table receive gradients, so the table must already be clear."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "gradient-descent",
        "reason": "The argument is about where gradients can and cannot flow."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "question",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Training adjusts the parameters of the network by following gradients. The tokenizer sits at the very start of the pipeline, turning text into token IDs. So is the tokenizer trained along with the network?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "No.",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " It is a fixed piece of preprocessing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 27)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "built-beforehand",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The tokenizer is "
            },
            {
              "text": "built beforehand",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " by a counting procedure over a text corpus, for example the merge counting of byte-pair encoding. Then it is "
            },
            {
              "text": "frozen",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The transformer is trained afterwards, on token IDs the frozen tokenizer produced."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 27)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "discrete",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "There is also a reason it could not simply be trained. Token IDs are "
            },
            {
              "text": "discrete",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": a piece of text is split one way or another, and there is nothing in between."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Gradients describe smooth changes, such as nudging a number a little. Choosing how to split text is not a smooth operation, so gradients cannot flow into the segmentation."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 28)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "rows",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Backpropagation does reach a little way past the ID: into the "
            },
            {
              "text": "embedding rows",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " that were looked up for those IDs. The row selected by each ID receives a gradient, and those rows are trained."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The segmentation itself, which decides which IDs appear at all, stays fixed."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 28)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "consequence",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because the vocabulary is fixed from corpus statistics, it fits some text better than other text. Text in languages that are under-represented in the tokenizer's training data typically needs more tokens per word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "That matters in practice, since context limits and API pricing are counted in tokens."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 26, 27)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "How is the tokenizer's vocabulary obtained, relative to training the model?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is built beforehand from corpus statistics, then frozen"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "For example by counting merges, as in byte-pair encoding."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is learned end to end by backpropagation together with the model"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Gradients cannot flow into how text is split."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is written by hand as a dictionary of common words"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is built by a counting procedure over a corpus."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is rebuilt after every training step"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "It is built once, before training, and then frozen."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["built-beforehand", "question"],
